<.shell current={:open_on_device}>
  <h1>Open on device</h1>
  <p class="lead">A QR code beside a page, to open it on a phone or iPad.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <div class="md:flex md:gap-8">
      <div class="md:w-2/3 min-w-0">
        <h2 class="heading">Your ID card</h2>
        <p class="lead mb-0">
          Cameron Ruiz, your ID card shows you are a current member of Coast Range SAR. Show it at the door, or to another team on a search.
        </p>
      </div>
      <.open_on_device
        id="open-on-device-demo"
        heading="Open on your phone"
        url="https://sarduty.com/cards/sample"
      >
        <p class="mb-0">
          Scan the code with your phone's camera, then add your ID card to Wallet there.
        </p>
      </.open_on_device>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    Beside a page whose task is done on another device, for someone who opened it on a computer: Take attendance on the door's iPad, and an ID card's page on the member's phone.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    A page people only open on the device that does the task, such as an activity's scanner. On a touch screen it is hidden, so it never shows there anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.open_on_device id="…" heading="Open on your phone" url=&#123;@url&#125;&gt;…&lt;/.open_on_device&gt;</code>. The heading says where it opens. The QR code holds <code>url</code>, the page's full address. The inner block says what to do with the code, then anything more, such as a
    <a href={page_path(:copy_button)}>copy button</a>
    for the address.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is the page's sidebar: put the main column in <code>md:w-2/3</code>, beside it in a
    <code>md:flex md:gap-8</code>
    row, and it takes <code>md:w-1/3</code>. On a phone it goes under the page. It is hidden on a touch screen, which is the device already, so view this page on a computer to see it.
  </p>
</.shell>
